WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:07.905
&gt;&gt; Hello, everyone. Welcome
to the first session.

00:00:07.906 --> 00:00:11.385
So, we'll have three talks
in that session.

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:13.620
First talk, Semantic
Matching Against

00:00:13.621 --> 00:00:15.900
Corpus: New Application
and Method,

00:00:15.901 --> 00:00:17.885
presented by Lucy Lin.

00:00:17.886 --> 00:00:21.165
So, the talk will be for
20 minutes. Thank you.

00:00:21.166 --> 00:00:23.190
&gt;&gt; Sweet. Can everyone
hear me okay?

00:00:23.191 --> 00:00:24.030
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:00:24.031 --> 00:00:26.460
&gt;&gt; Awesome. Thanks. Hi, I'm Lucy,

00:00:26.461 --> 00:00:28.960
and I'm going to
be talking about,

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:34.260
it will come back.

00:00:34.261 --> 00:00:36.320
Semantic matching
against text corpora,

00:00:36.321 --> 00:00:38.270
and this is in collaboration
with Scott Miles

00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:41.020
and Noah Smith at
the University of Washington.

00:00:41.021 --> 00:00:45.200
So, we've clearly made a
lot of recent advances

00:00:45.201 --> 00:00:47.075
in modeling sentential semantics

00:00:47.076 --> 00:00:49.440
like a lot of the analyte tasks,

00:00:49.441 --> 00:00:51.830
semantic similarity,
paraphrase, et cetera.

00:00:51.831 --> 00:00:53.370
So, what we going to

00:00:53.371 --> 00:00:55.235
propose is that
a natural thing to do

00:00:55.236 --> 00:00:56.990
is to match

00:00:56.991 --> 00:01:00.120
natural language propositions
against the corpus.

00:01:00.121 --> 00:01:04.480
We see possible end-users
as, for example,

00:01:04.481 --> 00:01:06.560
a historian of science
being interested in

00:01:06.561 --> 00:01:08.720
tracking the idea of
vaccines cause autism.

00:01:08.721 --> 00:01:12.865
You might expect
a spike in news after,

00:01:12.866 --> 00:01:17.130
say, the Andrew Wakefield
article about that.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.660
Similarly, political scientists
might be interested in

00:01:23.661 --> 00:01:27.790
how different framings
of policy issues occur,

00:01:27.791 --> 00:01:29.780
or public servants dealing

00:01:29.781 --> 00:01:31.610
with recovery after
natural disasters

00:01:31.611 --> 00:01:33.230
might be interested in

00:01:33.231 --> 00:01:36.225
ideas about well-being
or recovery.

00:01:36.226 --> 00:01:39.475
So, this is the use
case we imagine,

00:01:39.476 --> 00:01:41.675
where a user specifies a query

00:01:41.676 --> 00:01:42.800
like dealing with authorities

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:44.460
is causing stress and anxiety.

00:01:44.461 --> 00:01:46.410
We will query the corpus,

00:01:46.411 --> 00:01:48.400
return a list of match sentences.

00:01:48.401 --> 00:01:50.720
For example, those in
charge of recovery are

00:01:50.721 --> 00:01:53.810
making moves to appease
anger among homeowners.

00:01:53.811 --> 00:01:56.300
At the end of the talk, I'll
briefly touch on what we

00:01:56.301 --> 00:01:58.790
might be able to do with
a list of matched sentences,

00:01:58.791 --> 00:02:00.500
which is to maybe have

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:04.250
proxy measurement across
a variable like time.

00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:09.010
This is related to, as I
mentioned, work on entailment,

00:02:09.011 --> 00:02:11.090
semantics similarity, but there's

00:02:11.091 --> 00:02:13.430
also really work in
information retrieval,

00:02:13.431 --> 00:02:15.800
specifically passage retrieval
for question answering,

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:19.130
and also on the measurement side,

00:02:19.131 --> 00:02:20.650
it's similar in spirit to

00:02:20.651 --> 00:02:23.880
dynamic topic models
and related work.

00:02:23.881 --> 00:02:26.500
So, how is this going to go?

00:02:26.501 --> 00:02:30.395
I'm going to formalize this
a little bit, and then,

00:02:30.396 --> 00:02:32.650
talk about two different
applications: one in

00:02:32.651 --> 00:02:35.480
which we use examples
from a codebook,

00:02:35.481 --> 00:02:38.735
and this is the media
framing domain,

00:02:38.736 --> 00:02:39.980
and then, a second in which we

00:02:39.981 --> 00:02:41.670
have our domain expert, Scott,

00:02:41.671 --> 00:02:44.660
specify queries of ideas

00:02:44.661 --> 00:02:46.530
that he's interested
in disaster recovery,

00:02:46.531 --> 00:02:48.140
and then, we did a user study

00:02:48.141 --> 00:02:51.840
to validate that and see if
they're interested end users.

00:02:51.841 --> 00:02:53.420
Finally, I'll briefly discuss

00:02:53.421 --> 00:02:56.645
this using the output
for measurement idea.

00:02:56.646 --> 00:03:02.505
So, basically as inputs,

00:03:02.506 --> 00:03:04.310
we would have a
corpus of sentences,

00:03:04.311 --> 00:03:05.990
which we'll say C, and

00:03:05.991 --> 00:03:08.230
a proposition query containing
the idea of interests,

00:03:08.231 --> 00:03:10.005
which will call as P.

00:03:10.006 --> 00:03:12.640
We'll just treat
these as sentences.

00:03:12.641 --> 00:03:13.910
We're not going to consider

00:03:13.911 --> 00:03:15.085
document structure in this work,

00:03:15.086 --> 00:03:17.560
but would be interesting
in future work.

00:03:17.561 --> 00:03:20.090
What we'd like to do
is, basically score

00:03:20.091 --> 00:03:22.775
all these sentences
as in C such that

00:03:22.776 --> 00:03:25.280
the score should be high

00:03:25.281 --> 00:03:28.800
if and only if S expresses
the proposition query SP.

00:03:28.801 --> 00:03:31.185
From there, you can get
the top end sentences,

00:03:31.186 --> 00:03:35.320
and return out the list.

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:39.390
So, I'm going to talk about
this first application,

00:03:39.391 --> 00:03:44.760
where we're going to use
the Media Frames Corpus,

00:03:44.761 --> 00:03:47.120
which contains thousands of

00:03:47.121 --> 00:03:49.000
articles about
different policy issues.

00:03:49.001 --> 00:03:50.955
The one we're going to
focus on is immigration,

00:03:50.956 --> 00:03:52.850
and spans of texts

00:03:52.851 --> 00:03:54.895
are annotated with
different framing dimensions.

00:03:54.896 --> 00:03:57.560
These are things like, if

00:03:57.561 --> 00:03:59.040
the issue is
constant legal terms,

00:03:59.041 --> 00:04:02.680
and economic terms, quality
of life, and so on.

00:04:02.681 --> 00:04:06.500
We also have the annotation
codebook for that corpus,

00:04:06.501 --> 00:04:07.820
and we're going to take

00:04:07.821 --> 00:04:09.620
30 annotation codebook examples

00:04:09.621 --> 00:04:11.905
as the ideas of interests.

00:04:11.906 --> 00:04:14.540
One example of this
is immigration rules

00:04:14.541 --> 00:04:16.790
have changed on
fairly over time which invokes

00:04:16.791 --> 00:04:19.070
this framing of
fairness and equality

00:04:19.071 --> 00:04:22.210
with relation to immigration.

00:04:22.211 --> 00:04:25.790
So the idea basically
is that if we

00:04:25.791 --> 00:04:30.280
match an example sentence
from the codebook,

00:04:30.281 --> 00:04:34.975
then it should evoke the frame
that we're expecting,

00:04:34.976 --> 00:04:36.650
that it's an example of.

00:04:36.651 --> 00:04:37.940
Just as a side note,
there are many ways

00:04:37.941 --> 00:04:39.150
to invoke a frame
outside the codebooks,

00:04:39.151 --> 00:04:43.000
so we're not really looking
for like high recall at all ,

00:04:43.001 --> 00:04:46.090
we're just looking
at precision here.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:50.960
So because we're evoking frames

00:04:50.961 --> 00:04:52.840
and it's a broad category

00:04:52.841 --> 00:04:55.040
where the user really
simple scoring function.

00:04:55.041 --> 00:04:56.530
Basically, what we're going to

00:04:56.531 --> 00:04:58.010
do is on each sentence we will

00:04:58.011 --> 00:05:00.890
represent as the average
of its word vectors,

00:05:00.891 --> 00:05:02.930
and then we're going to
take the cosine similarity

00:05:02.931 --> 00:05:04.395
between the two sentence vectors,

00:05:04.396 --> 00:05:08.770
and that's going to be
the scoring function.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:14.060
We try out two
different word vector

00:05:14.061 --> 00:05:15.840
variants that are pretrained.

00:05:15.841 --> 00:05:19.970
One are paraphrastic word
vectors by waiting at all,

00:05:19.971 --> 00:05:21.920
and these are trained on the PDB.

00:05:21.921 --> 00:05:25.320
They're designed to be used
in these similarity tasks.

00:05:25.321 --> 00:05:28.965
The other is the very
well-known Word2Vec

00:05:28.966 --> 00:05:30.895
pre-trained on Google News,

00:05:30.896 --> 00:05:32.950
and the nice thing about this is

00:05:32.951 --> 00:05:35.280
that because they're
trained on structured data,

00:05:35.281 --> 00:05:38.340
they might have a lot of
entities we're interested in.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.270
So, we're interested
in how well does

00:05:41.271 --> 00:05:45.380
our output line up with
the corpus annotations.

00:05:45.381 --> 00:05:47.835
So, we look at this at
the sentence level where

00:05:47.836 --> 00:05:51.715
if we match this sentence,

00:05:51.716 --> 00:05:55.015
does the frame line up
with the annotated frame?

00:05:55.016 --> 00:05:56.360
What we find is basically

00:05:56.361 --> 00:05:58.940
that the paraphrastic
vectors do better

00:05:58.941 --> 00:06:00.800
than the Word2vec vectors
which in turn do

00:06:00.801 --> 00:06:03.220
better than just a
TF-IDF baseline.

00:06:03.221 --> 00:06:05.520
So we're not really looking for,

00:06:05.521 --> 00:06:07.310
they're clearly probably
better methods to

00:06:07.311 --> 00:06:08.930
do this to get
much higher precision,

00:06:08.931 --> 00:06:12.380
but this is a first step,

00:06:12.381 --> 00:06:15.230
and does really well
given how simple it is.

00:06:15.231 --> 00:06:21.790
So, there we go.

00:06:21.791 --> 00:06:23.930
Secondly, I'm going to talk

00:06:23.931 --> 00:06:27.125
about matching
more specific queries.

00:06:27.126 --> 00:06:30.860
This is going to be in
a domain where researchers and

00:06:30.861 --> 00:06:31.940
public servants don't necessarily

00:06:31.941 --> 00:06:34.825
have lot empirical data,

00:06:34.826 --> 00:06:36.200
and they want to understand what

00:06:36.201 --> 00:06:37.640
challenges the
community is facing as

00:06:37.641 --> 00:06:43.860
a postmortem for
post-disaster work.

00:06:43.861 --> 00:06:48.560
So, our thinking is that we
can use these techniques to

00:06:48.561 --> 00:06:51.200
find relevant sentences from

00:06:51.201 --> 00:06:53.870
which they can,
fortunately, learn from.

00:06:53.871 --> 00:06:55.940
So we're going to
specifically focus on

00:06:55.941 --> 00:07:00.005
the Canterbury earthquakes
New Zealand from 2010,2011.

00:07:00.006 --> 00:07:02.000
We pulled a corpus

00:07:02.001 --> 00:07:10.425
of about a thousand news articles

00:07:10.426 --> 00:07:13.455
that are from local news sources,

00:07:13.456 --> 00:07:16.870
and as ideas of interest,

00:07:16.871 --> 00:07:18.950
we had our domain expert, Scott,

00:07:18.951 --> 00:07:20.930
provide 20 queries like

00:07:20.931 --> 00:07:22.925
he's interested in
for his own work.

00:07:22.926 --> 00:07:24.200
These are things
covering community

00:07:24.201 --> 00:07:25.390
well being, infrastructural,

00:07:25.391 --> 00:07:28.690
decision-making utilities, so on.

00:07:28.691 --> 00:07:29.750
For example,
the council should have

00:07:29.751 --> 00:07:31.680
consulted residents
before making decisions,

00:07:31.681 --> 00:07:34.050
seems obvious apparently it's
not to certain governments.

00:07:34.051 --> 00:07:37.900
So, in this case,

00:07:37.901 --> 00:07:39.950
unlike in the framing
case we're really

00:07:39.951 --> 00:07:41.840
interested in more
fine-grained matching,

00:07:41.841 --> 00:07:44.100
really went to match this idea.

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.850
So, because of that,
we're going to

00:07:48.851 --> 00:07:52.700
use a more entailment model.

00:07:52.701 --> 00:07:55.010
It's going to be based on syntax,

00:07:55.011 --> 00:07:57.955
and I'm going to walk
through very quickly.

00:07:57.956 --> 00:08:00.180
So, given two sentences,

00:08:00.181 --> 00:08:02.095
the candidate sentence
from the corpus,

00:08:02.096 --> 00:08:05.010
and the proposition query,

00:08:05.011 --> 00:08:08.130
we're going to get
the dependency parses of each.

00:08:08.131 --> 00:08:11.900
We're then going to find
a sequence of what we're calling

00:08:11.901 --> 00:08:13.700
tree edit operations
which transform

00:08:13.701 --> 00:08:14.950
one tree into the other.

00:08:14.951 --> 00:08:17.495
So, the intuition
basically is that

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:19.820
the difference between
the two sentence is

00:08:19.821 --> 00:08:21.170
somehow indicative
of the relationship

00:08:21.171 --> 00:08:22.185
between the tailor fits,

00:08:22.186 --> 00:08:24.530
if they match each
other, if they don't,

00:08:24.531 --> 00:08:27.170
and this syntactic transformation

00:08:27.171 --> 00:08:29.840
is one way of getting at that.

00:08:29.841 --> 00:08:33.514
To be more clear, we
can take this example

00:08:33.515 --> 00:08:37.220
where we start with

00:08:37.221 --> 00:08:38.660
the sentence
unfamiliar bureaucratic

00:08:38.661 --> 00:08:40.135
systems are causing stress,

00:08:40.136 --> 00:08:41.660
and we want to find a set of

00:08:41.661 --> 00:08:44.950
transformations such that we

00:08:44.951 --> 00:08:47.340
end up with this tree
at the bottom,

00:08:47.341 --> 00:08:51.035
and so you might want to
delete a couple of the words,

00:08:51.036 --> 00:08:56.135
relabel, and then finally
you insert to get the entry.

00:08:56.136 --> 00:08:58.180
I'm not going to go into
details about how we do this,

00:08:58.181 --> 00:09:00.440
but it's basically a research.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:03.830
From there, we use that sequence

00:09:03.831 --> 00:09:06.715
as features into a classifier,

00:09:06.716 --> 00:09:09.290
the original paper
from Heilman and Smith

00:09:09.291 --> 00:09:11.270
that we worked off of use

00:09:11.271 --> 00:09:14.415
logistic regression
classifier with

00:09:14.416 --> 00:09:16.055
based on counts of features.

00:09:16.056 --> 00:09:18.455
This is 2018, so

00:09:18.456 --> 00:09:20.300
we're going to also put
into in neural networks.

00:09:20.301 --> 00:09:23.740
It's a sequence and
we should do that.

00:09:23.741 --> 00:09:25.145
We just trained it on as SNLI,

00:09:25.146 --> 00:09:28.440
stands for Natural
Language Inference Corpus,

00:09:28.441 --> 00:09:34.590
and just two class entail
versus neutral contradiction.

00:09:34.591 --> 00:09:37.820
So, to make this run in
a reasonable amount of time,

00:09:37.821 --> 00:09:42.865
we first do this fast word
vector based matching

00:09:42.866 --> 00:09:45.750
on entire corpus obtain

00:09:45.751 --> 00:09:47.300
some subset of matches from which

00:09:47.301 --> 00:09:48.910
we then run this
entailment based model,

00:09:48.911 --> 00:09:51.610
and we tried out
different combinations of these.

00:09:51.611 --> 00:09:55.475
So, to evaluate, we survey
20 emergency managers,

00:09:55.476 --> 00:09:59.430
and took the output of
the different systems,

00:09:59.431 --> 00:10:01.460
and had them rate
from one to five

00:10:01.461 --> 00:10:05.150
how good of a match they were
to come here to the query.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:07.390
So, an example that we showed

00:10:07.391 --> 00:10:10.505
them is there's a shortage
of construction workers,

00:10:10.506 --> 00:10:13.600
and so one is like this is not

00:10:13.601 --> 00:10:15.100
really expressing idea at all

00:10:15.101 --> 00:10:17.770
to five which is,
"Hey, this is great."

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.130
So, what we see is
that on the left are,

00:10:22.131 --> 00:10:24.280
the left two are basically we use

00:10:24.281 --> 00:10:27.860
the paraphrastic vectors in
this like first parse filter,

00:10:27.861 --> 00:10:32.950
and then they're between
the two sides they are

00:10:32.951 --> 00:10:36.550
the logistic regression
Ellis TM versions

00:10:36.551 --> 00:10:39.320
of the syntax-based model.

00:10:39.321 --> 00:10:41.380
In general, the
paraphrastic vectors

00:10:41.381 --> 00:10:43.300
again do better
the word [inaudible] ,

00:10:43.301 --> 00:10:46.235
and this makes a difference
on what you do afterwards.

00:10:46.236 --> 00:10:47.990
Basically, if you have

00:10:47.991 --> 00:10:50.325
mediocre output from
that first parse,

00:10:50.326 --> 00:10:52.970
you're not really going
to make things better.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:56.299
We also asked respondents

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:59.740
if they're just interested
in this application.

00:10:59.741 --> 00:11:03.045
In general, most of them
said yes, which is great,

00:11:03.046 --> 00:11:05.360
and then half of them were
actually interested in

00:11:05.361 --> 00:11:07.995
a follow-up study which is
it progress, and basically,

00:11:07.996 --> 00:11:10.820
we're having them specify
their own idea of queries,

00:11:10.821 --> 00:11:12.660
and so it's like this back and

00:11:12.661 --> 00:11:15.680
forth study, which is cool.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:21.595
So, finally, I'm going
to talk about using

00:11:21.596 --> 00:11:28.910
this output in like
a measurement.

00:11:29.280 --> 00:11:32.540
What I mean by that is, say,

00:11:32.541 --> 00:11:34.840
we have this query,

00:11:34.841 --> 00:11:37.330
"Dealing with authorities is
causing stress and anxiety."

00:11:37.331 --> 00:11:39.930
That's been kind of using
throughout this talk,

00:11:39.931 --> 00:11:43.190
and if we take.

00:11:43.191 --> 00:11:46.255
&gt;&gt; It's supposed to be fixed.

00:11:46.256 --> 00:11:49.495
&gt;&gt; It's okay. There we go.

00:11:49.496 --> 00:11:54.445
If we take the top 50 sentences,

00:11:54.446 --> 00:11:57.540
we look at the metadata for
the articles they came from.

00:11:57.541 --> 00:11:59.575
They're published
on certain date,

00:11:59.576 --> 00:12:02.470
which kind of indicates, oh, hi.

00:12:02.471 --> 00:12:05.230
This idea showing
up at this time.

00:12:05.231 --> 00:12:06.310
If we've done this
in three months,

00:12:06.311 --> 00:12:08.950
we get the following histogram.

00:12:08.951 --> 00:12:10.800
So, we showed this guy and he

00:12:10.801 --> 00:12:13.090
was like, "This is interesting."

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:17.105
That's kind of worrisome.
Unfortunately.

00:12:17.106 --> 00:12:19.135
&gt;&gt; They're trying to fix it.

00:12:19.136 --> 00:12:21.770
&gt;&gt; It's like Window reboot.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:26.720
Basically, after
the earthquake happens,

00:12:26.721 --> 00:12:28.685
which was kind of 0.11ish.

00:12:28.686 --> 00:12:30.425
People are still kind,

00:12:30.426 --> 00:12:31.780
they are feeling okay

00:12:31.781 --> 00:12:33.680
about the fact that
they're getting help,

00:12:33.681 --> 00:12:35.130
the governments they're involved

00:12:35.131 --> 00:12:41.335
in giving aid in terms of
recovery, services et cetera.

00:12:41.336 --> 00:12:44.040
As time goes on,

00:12:44.041 --> 00:12:46.475
worry and stress starts to set in

00:12:46.476 --> 00:12:49.315
because their needs
aren't being fulfilled,

00:12:49.316 --> 00:12:50.710
or they feel like
they're being forgotten,

00:12:50.711 --> 00:12:55.130
and so that translates
into subsequent coverage.

00:12:55.590 --> 00:12:59.350
So, some things that we found for

00:12:59.351 --> 00:13:04.825
both studies that kind of
motivate future work are,

00:13:04.826 --> 00:13:08.105
one, the complexity of that idea.

00:13:08.106 --> 00:13:11.145
So, sometimes queries that are
too general, too specific.

00:13:11.146 --> 00:13:14.460
That impacts, the kinds of
matched sentences you get,

00:13:14.461 --> 00:13:16.380
and also can we give user guides

00:13:16.381 --> 00:13:18.780
for maybe writing better queries.

00:13:18.781 --> 00:13:22.610
So, that's kind of progress
with this follow-up study.

00:13:22.611 --> 00:13:24.730
So, another problem we

00:13:24.731 --> 00:13:27.820
ran into was,
Entities/Coreference.

00:13:27.821 --> 00:13:30.100
This is particularly
interesting in

00:13:30.101 --> 00:13:33.080
New Zealand earthquake data

00:13:33.081 --> 00:13:36.400
because a lot of those entities
just they don't,

00:13:36.401 --> 00:13:39.580
they're local, they don't
really show up in our news.

00:13:39.581 --> 00:13:41.340
An example of this
is CERA which is

00:13:41.341 --> 00:13:44.580
the Canterbury Earthquake
Recovery Authority.

00:13:44.581 --> 00:13:47.915
There's apparently also
the last name of an actor who,

00:13:47.916 --> 00:13:50.670
I didn't know existed.

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:56.260
Finally, there just, the
fact that we're using,

00:13:56.261 --> 00:13:58.710
we're kind of breaking
off sentence boundaries

00:13:58.711 --> 00:14:01.160
is a limiting factor.

00:14:01.161 --> 00:14:02.340
So, in two ways.

00:14:02.341 --> 00:14:05.115
One, discerning cut contexts
can invalidate a match.

00:14:05.116 --> 00:14:07.935
Secondly, potential match can

00:14:07.936 --> 00:14:11.380
be spread across a sentence
boundary, so we'll miss it.

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:15.395
So, in future work, it
would be interesting to

00:14:15.396 --> 00:14:18.890
incorporate that or
document structure, so on.

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:24.160
So, just to wrap up,

00:14:24.161 --> 00:14:27.360
in this talk we showed
the kind of viability of

00:14:27.361 --> 00:14:28.810
different semantic
matching methods and

00:14:28.811 --> 00:14:31.305
two pretty different
application domains.

00:14:31.306 --> 00:14:34.270
We performed a user
study to establish

00:14:34.271 --> 00:14:35.750
some end user interest at least

00:14:35.751 --> 00:14:37.570
in this particular application,

00:14:37.571 --> 00:14:39.710
and motivated future work on

00:14:39.711 --> 00:14:42.390
semantic matching and
other measurement applications.

