here are our actual names.

00:20:25.741 --> 00:20:28.535
So, the sort of basic,

00:20:28.536 --> 00:20:30.560
placed with a situates
in the literature right,

00:20:30.561 --> 00:20:32.720
is in this context of things
like adversarial examples.

00:20:32.721 --> 00:20:33.740
We're very familiar with

00:20:33.741 --> 00:20:36.810
this classic example from
the vision literature, right?

00:20:36.811 --> 00:20:39.190
Where you have an image which
was classified as a panda,

00:20:39.191 --> 00:20:40.795
you add what is seemingly sort of

00:20:40.796 --> 00:20:43.455
imperceptible noise and now
you have a misclassification.

00:20:43.456 --> 00:20:44.950
The question becomes, sort of,

00:20:44.951 --> 00:20:47.880
what does that look like
for natural language?

00:20:47.881 --> 00:20:50.260
What's important right
is that the noise

00:20:50.261 --> 00:20:52.535
distorts the perceptual
process in some way,

00:20:52.536 --> 00:20:53.280
but it doesn't actually

00:20:53.281 --> 00:20:54.520
hinder the semantic
understanding.

00:20:54.521 --> 00:20:55.660
So, maybe you did notice that

00:20:55.661 --> 00:20:57.190
the second image was
a little bit blurrier,

00:20:57.191 --> 00:20:58.995
but it didn't actually
affect your ability to

00:20:58.996 --> 00:21:00.900
understand what it was.

00:21:00.901 --> 00:21:03.825
This is not a typical right
of our perceptual systems,

00:21:03.826 --> 00:21:05.440
for example, our eyes
also caught all over

00:21:05.441 --> 00:21:07.400
the place, all the time.

00:21:07.401 --> 00:21:09.060
It sort of gives you
a little bit of that,

00:21:09.061 --> 00:21:10.070
this is not in the paper, but

00:21:10.071 --> 00:21:11.745
some work we've been
doing since then.

00:21:11.746 --> 00:21:14.230
If you take some texts,

00:21:14.231 --> 00:21:15.860
you go ahead and introduce

00:21:15.861 --> 00:21:18.265
the same kind of errors
that I showed earlier.

00:21:18.266 --> 00:21:19.860
So, here we have
a lawsuit, was filed

00:21:19.861 --> 00:21:22.155
March 27th and the
preliminary investigation,

00:21:22.156 --> 00:21:23.715
was opened by the prosecutors.

00:21:23.716 --> 00:21:25.190
First of all, it's
important to note that

00:21:25.191 --> 00:21:27.355
no one has any trouble
reading this.

00:21:27.356 --> 00:21:29.190
Just to prove that point,

00:21:29.191 --> 00:21:30.850
we went ahead and
run an eye tracker.

00:21:30.851 --> 00:21:31.930
So, we had people sit down

00:21:31.931 --> 00:21:33.080
and actually reading
this sentence.

00:21:33.081 --> 00:21:35.065
So, we have this sort
of fix it here,

00:21:35.066 --> 00:21:36.670
they jump a little bit,

00:21:36.671 --> 00:21:39.080
move forward the entire phrase,

00:21:39.081 --> 00:21:42.765
backtrack and then move through
the rest of the sentence.

00:21:42.766 --> 00:21:44.610
The only reason this is really

00:21:44.611 --> 00:21:46.930
important is that
there are 14 words,

00:21:46.931 --> 00:21:48.565
there are nine fixations.

00:21:48.566 --> 00:21:50.420
So, when people are

00:21:50.421 --> 00:21:52.160
reading this text
and we actually have

00:21:52.161 --> 00:21:53.790
them answering
questions at the end to

00:21:53.791 --> 00:21:55.860
make sure that they did
comprehend the text,

00:21:55.861 --> 00:21:57.910
they're not paying a lot

00:21:57.911 --> 00:21:59.060
of attention to a
lot of the words.

00:21:59.061 --> 00:22:00.860
In fact, based on what we know

00:22:00.861 --> 00:22:03.130
about how much you
see when you look at

00:22:03.131 --> 00:22:05.145
a specific part of the sentence

00:22:05.146 --> 00:22:06.410
would probably happened in

00:22:06.411 --> 00:22:07.800
this fixation as they saw

00:22:07.801 --> 00:22:09.450
the entire word completely

00:22:09.451 --> 00:22:11.210
skipped was, and then moved on.

00:22:11.211 --> 00:22:12.840
Right. So they
probably didn't even

00:22:12.841 --> 00:22:14.550
notice which we
see, for instance,

00:22:14.551 --> 00:22:15.790
there are some
interesting work in

00:22:15.791 --> 00:22:17.450
the psych literature where
you interview people,

00:22:17.451 --> 00:22:18.770
didn't even notice that
there was an error

00:22:18.771 --> 00:22:20.700
there. We do this
all the time, right?

00:22:20.701 --> 00:22:22.625
We make mistakes in
our own writing,

00:22:22.626 --> 00:22:24.770
we then reread it
and totally miss it.

00:22:24.771 --> 00:22:26.965
We need a new pair of
eyes to notice it.

00:22:26.966 --> 00:22:30.215
So, perception is fundamentally
noisy and sparse.

00:22:30.216 --> 00:22:32.220
So, maybe the reason
that we aren't

00:22:32.221 --> 00:22:34.400
disturbed by these kinds
of adversarial examples,

00:22:34.401 --> 00:22:35.630
is because we've been
sort of training

00:22:35.631 --> 00:22:36.855
on it our entire lives.

00:22:36.856 --> 00:22:38.300
Another place you
might be training on

00:22:38.301 --> 00:22:40.560
these things is
messy handwriting, right?

00:22:40.561 --> 00:22:42.360
Your entire life you're
trying to decipher

00:22:42.361 --> 00:22:43.950
doctor's notes or other kinds

00:22:43.951 --> 00:22:45.475
of things maybe your child wrote.

00:22:45.476 --> 00:22:48.000
You may yourself have
really messy handwriting and,

00:22:48.001 --> 00:22:50.300
so I went ahead and
did some research,

00:22:50.301 --> 00:22:52.410
I went online and I searched and

00:22:52.411 --> 00:22:54.875
it looks like if you
have messy handwriting,

00:22:54.876 --> 00:22:56.685
you might have emotional baggage.

00:22:56.686 --> 00:22:59.560
So, that, you know,
just take that home.

00:22:59.561 --> 00:23:01.970
On the other hand, I did
continue searching and if you

00:23:01.971 --> 00:23:03.180
go further down on

00:23:03.181 --> 00:23:04.565
the results you might
also be a genius.

00:23:04.566 --> 00:23:07.330
So, it's a little unclear what
the research shows there,

00:23:07.331 --> 00:23:09.750
but either way you've
probably been exposed to

00:23:09.751 --> 00:23:12.560
noisy texts throughout your life.

00:23:12.561 --> 00:23:14.500
So, what we're doing
in this setup is I'm

00:23:14.501 --> 00:23:16.610
just going to give
you The Super Quick.

00:23:16.611 --> 00:23:17.925
We have a bunch of models

00:23:17.926 --> 00:23:19.180
which we're going to test and see

00:23:19.181 --> 00:23:22.195
how brittle they are and
how noise affects them.

00:23:22.196 --> 00:23:23.960
So, first we use Nematus which is

00:23:23.961 --> 00:23:26.560
the machine translation framework

00:23:26.561 --> 00:23:28.880
which perform very
well in a bunch

00:23:28.881 --> 00:23:31.625
of WMT tasks from last year.

00:23:31.626 --> 00:23:34.690
It's a BPE based representations,
if you're not familiar,

00:23:34.691 --> 00:23:36.850
that just means that
instead of using words as

00:23:36.851 --> 00:23:40.060
input and sort of finding
sets of characters,

00:23:40.061 --> 00:23:42.020
that for instance, like
a morphological ending like

00:23:42.021 --> 00:23:43.935
ING gets chunked out.

00:23:43.936 --> 00:23:46.145
So, this is a really
helpful way of

00:23:46.146 --> 00:23:49.070
increasing robustness to
out-of-vocabulary effects.

00:23:49.071 --> 00:23:51.610
We're then also going
to use this work

00:23:51.611 --> 00:23:53.680
from Taco last year
chart-to-chart.

00:23:53.681 --> 00:23:56.490
This is a very impressive
model that doesn't

00:23:56.491 --> 00:23:59.390
even take into account
segmentation information.

00:23:59.391 --> 00:24:01.130
So, it just treats
the entire input as

00:24:01.131 --> 00:24:02.310
one big character stream and

00:24:02.311 --> 00:24:04.310
then translates to characters.

00:24:04.311 --> 00:24:06.063
Then finally, just as

00:24:06.064 --> 00:24:08.970
a simple baseline what you
might code up in an afternoon.

00:24:08.971 --> 00:24:12.450
So, this is your sequence-to-sequence
model with attention

00:24:12.451 --> 00:24:15.230
and character-based convolutions
for the representation.

00:24:15.231 --> 00:24:16.630
So, it's not super important.

00:24:16.631 --> 00:24:19.770
The thing to keep in mind is
just that this final model

00:24:19.771 --> 00:24:21.360
here and has notions of

00:24:21.361 --> 00:24:22.510
what word boundaries are but

00:24:22.511 --> 00:24:23.960
looks at individual characters.

00:24:23.961 --> 00:24:26.520
This model doesn't even
care about word boundaries,

00:24:26.521 --> 00:24:28.060
and this top model has

00:24:28.061 --> 00:24:30.460
maybe slightly more
intelligent ways of

00:24:30.461 --> 00:24:33.760
figuring out morphological
units and so forth.

00:24:33.761 --> 00:24:35.345
Then for the data,
we're going to use

00:24:35.346 --> 00:24:36.755
a spoken text corpus.

00:24:36.756 --> 00:24:40.725
So, the IWSLT competition
in couple of years back,

00:24:40.726 --> 00:24:42.160
they had a bunch
of these Ted Talks

00:24:42.161 --> 00:24:43.265
and their translations.

00:24:43.266 --> 00:24:44.810
So, that's what we're
going to do the evaluation

00:24:44.811 --> 00:24:46.740
and then also some of
the training I'll show you.

00:24:46.741 --> 00:24:48.815
So, first let's talk
about synthetic errors.

00:24:48.816 --> 00:24:50.300
So, the beauty of adversarial

00:24:50.301 --> 00:24:51.310
examples that exist,
for instance,

00:24:51.311 --> 00:24:52.480
in the vision
literature is that you

00:24:52.481 --> 00:24:54.240
basically can just
sample random noise.

00:24:54.241 --> 00:24:55.890
So, what would that
look like for us if we

00:24:55.891 --> 00:24:57.910
wanted to just automatically
create errors?

00:24:57.911 --> 00:24:59.230
Well, something we
do all the time is

00:24:59.231 --> 00:25:00.560
we swap two letters, right?

00:25:00.561 --> 00:25:01.760
You're typing, you
type too quickly,

00:25:01.761 --> 00:25:02.735
you flip two letters.

00:25:02.736 --> 00:25:04.260
So, we're going to
restrict that to being in

00:25:04.261 --> 00:25:06.520
longer words and only in
the middle of the word,

00:25:06.521 --> 00:25:07.980
say, sort of make
it a little easier

00:25:07.981 --> 00:25:11.295
but noise goes to
nosey or something.

00:25:11.296 --> 00:25:13.930
There's a meme that you might
remember from many years

00:25:13.931 --> 00:25:15.850
back according to
research at Cambridge.

00:25:15.851 --> 00:25:17.190
If you scramble
all the letters in

00:25:17.191 --> 00:25:18.860
this word or something
to this effect,

00:25:18.861 --> 00:25:20.190
you can still read it as long as

00:25:20.191 --> 00:25:21.850
the first and last
letters stay put.

00:25:21.851 --> 00:25:23.020
So, we're going to pretend like

00:25:23.021 --> 00:25:24.190
the Internet knows things,

00:25:24.191 --> 00:25:26.300
and we're going to go ahead and

00:25:26.301 --> 00:25:28.740
follow the meme for
our second type of noise.

00:25:28.741 --> 00:25:31.490
Third, what if we just
ignore that and randomly

00:25:31.491 --> 00:25:33.860
permute all the letters in
the word and then finally,

00:25:33.861 --> 00:25:36.270
all the time we end up
hitting a key by accident.

00:25:36.271 --> 00:25:38.520
So, what happens if we swap
one of the letters with

00:25:38.521 --> 00:25:42.095
one which is within a radius
one key on the keyboard?

00:25:42.096 --> 00:25:45.370
The effect of this on
these two on the, sort of,

00:25:45.371 --> 00:25:49.280
the two pre-trained, state
of the art fancy models is,

00:25:49.281 --> 00:25:51.560
that as you change the number
of tokens that are

00:25:51.561 --> 00:25:54.365
permuted or that are messed up,

00:25:54.366 --> 00:25:56.655
you see the bleu scores drop off.

00:25:56.656 --> 00:26:00.720
Not terribly surprising.
We see, for example,

00:26:00.721 --> 00:26:02.990
is that by the time we get to
something like 20 percent,

00:26:02.991 --> 00:26:05.420
we've actually lost almost half

00:26:05.421 --> 00:26:06.805
of our performance, right?

00:26:06.806 --> 00:26:08.910
Ten percent is still

00:26:08.911 --> 00:26:11.190
a pretty steep drop-off
in both of these models.

00:26:11.191 --> 00:26:14.900
So, it doesn't take a lot
to mess with translation.

00:26:14.901 --> 00:26:16.230
I'll come back to what we might

00:26:16.231 --> 00:26:17.860
do about it a little bit later.

00:26:17.861 --> 00:26:20.235
The second thing you
might ask is, well,

00:26:20.236 --> 00:26:21.880
these are some nice
synthetic results,

00:26:21.881 --> 00:26:23.080
what does it look
like if we actually

00:26:23.081 --> 00:26:24.370
try to use real noise?

00:26:24.371 --> 00:26:26.665
So I said we have some Wiki
edits and stuff like this.

00:26:26.666 --> 00:26:28.600
So, it turns out that there are

00:26:28.601 --> 00:26:31.910
some beautiful people out
there that have created

00:26:31.911 --> 00:26:35.040
these collections as
sort of second language speakers

00:26:35.041 --> 00:26:37.840
in their edits to
them writing essays,

00:26:37.841 --> 00:26:39.965
Wikipedia corpora
stuff like this.

00:26:39.966 --> 00:26:41.760
So, we can go and we
can find a bunch of

00:26:41.761 --> 00:26:43.450
naturally occurring
errors in the wild,

00:26:43.451 --> 00:26:46.215
and then we can use them
to permute our text.

00:26:46.216 --> 00:26:47.850
I don't speak a word of German,

00:26:47.851 --> 00:26:51.340
but I'm told that what's
going on here is that,

00:26:51.341 --> 00:26:54.175
for instance, we have phonetic
distinctions like T and D,

00:26:54.176 --> 00:26:57.365
S and Z are getting screwed up.

00:26:57.366 --> 00:26:58.740
We have dropped letters like

00:26:58.741 --> 00:27:01.520
the T missing and
babysitter here.

00:27:01.521 --> 00:27:03.120
Then we kind of get into

00:27:03.121 --> 00:27:05.010
the more interesting space which

00:27:05.011 --> 00:27:07.240
is things like morphological
problems, right?

00:27:07.241 --> 00:27:10.285
So, where someone has conjugated
something incorrectly.

00:27:10.286 --> 00:27:12.170
This is the kind of
stuff where if we

00:27:12.171 --> 00:27:14.040
really want to be able to
do this automatically,

00:27:14.041 --> 00:27:15.365
we're going to have
to have a much,

00:27:15.366 --> 00:27:18.030
maybe, stronger notion
of linguistics to do

00:27:18.031 --> 00:27:19.380
it than the kind of simple

00:27:19.381 --> 00:27:20.990
errors I was presenting before.

00:27:20.991 --> 00:27:23.685
So, the question becomes,

00:27:23.686 --> 00:27:26.160
''Do these errors affect

00:27:26.161 --> 00:27:27.520
our models in the same way

00:27:27.521 --> 00:27:28.970
that our synthetic errors do?''

00:27:28.971 --> 00:27:30.860
Yes. So, all I've done here,

00:27:30.861 --> 00:27:33.930
is I've gone ahead
and superimposed,

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:38.180
this is the natural error line
on both of these.

00:27:38.181 --> 00:27:39.850
It's just a hair better,

00:27:39.851 --> 00:27:41.620
so maybe it's the case
that there are

00:27:41.621 --> 00:27:43.480
certain types of errors
that are less likely

00:27:43.481 --> 00:27:45.860
to be made then just

00:27:45.861 --> 00:27:48.980
willy nilly swapping,
randomizing of letters.

00:27:48.981 --> 00:27:50.830
So, for example,
maybe people are less

00:27:50.831 --> 00:27:53.385
likely to mess up
a capitalization,

00:27:53.386 --> 00:27:56.080
maybe people are less likely
to mess up, you know,

00:27:56.081 --> 00:27:58.230
they may choose the wrong
conjugation but at least

00:27:58.231 --> 00:28:00.500
it's a valid conjugation,
these kinds of things, right?

00:28:00.501 --> 00:28:01.730
So, there's a quite a bit more

00:28:01.731 --> 00:28:03.190
exploration that's
necessary there.

00:28:03.191 --> 00:28:04.840
The other thing is
that we don't have

00:28:04.841 --> 00:28:07.020
full token coverage because,

00:28:07.021 --> 00:28:09.070
for example, we just don't

00:28:09.071 --> 00:28:10.930
have errors for
every single word.

00:28:10.931 --> 00:28:14.050
So, this maxes out
at about 40 percent.

00:28:14.051 --> 00:28:18.155
Okay, so, as I started
with spell checkers.

00:28:18.156 --> 00:28:21.670
So, like, obviously I said
they're not perfect but,

00:28:21.671 --> 00:28:24.064
like, they're probably
like not terrible.

00:28:24.065 --> 00:28:27.015
So, here's what happens
with our French,

00:28:27.016 --> 00:28:28.614
German and Czech models,

00:28:28.615 --> 00:28:31.840
if we just introduce
a bunch of natural errors.

00:28:31.841 --> 00:28:33.335
So, here are our bleu scores.

00:28:33.336 --> 00:28:35.420
Here's what happens
if we just select

00:28:35.421 --> 00:28:37.715
the first spell
checker suggestion

00:28:37.716 --> 00:28:42.420
for every single word in
the air-filled corpus.

00:28:42.421 --> 00:28:43.910
So, what you first note is that,

00:28:43.911 --> 00:28:45.170
first, French and German,

00:28:45.171 --> 00:28:46.780
we are seeing
pretty significant gains,

00:28:46.781 --> 00:28:49.495
something that unifies
six bleu points.

00:28:49.496 --> 00:28:51.565
So, spell checkers
are doing something.

00:28:51.566 --> 00:28:54.685
The other thing though that
I should mention is that,

00:28:54.686 --> 00:28:56.500
in the case of French and German,

00:28:56.501 --> 00:28:58.330
you're only seeing typically

00:28:58.331 --> 00:28:59.860
one to two suggestions from

00:28:59.861 --> 00:29:02.695
the spell checker for each
one of those recommendations.

00:29:02.696 --> 00:29:05.050
That is not the case
for Czech, right?

00:29:05.051 --> 00:29:06.860
So, when you look at
the Czech errors,

00:29:06.861 --> 00:29:07.940
what ends up happening is,

00:29:07.941 --> 00:29:10.070
you have this insane list

00:29:10.071 --> 00:29:12.520
of all the things that
could possibly happen.

00:29:12.521 --> 00:29:14.555
Again, I don't speak any Czech,

00:29:14.556 --> 00:29:16.265
so I just picked the first one.

00:29:16.266 --> 00:29:18.000
It turns out that actually

00:29:18.001 --> 00:29:19.630
on average I made things worse.

00:29:19.631 --> 00:29:22.465
So, that's sort of not,

00:29:22.466 --> 00:29:25.180
you know, maybe for
the small error condition,

00:29:25.181 --> 00:29:26.810
given sufficient contexts a rich,

00:29:26.811 --> 00:29:29.010
spell checker would
be beneficial,

00:29:29.011 --> 00:29:30.605
but those are also
going to break down.

00:29:30.606 --> 00:29:31.740
Then just as a reminder, these

00:29:31.741 --> 00:29:33.575
are still quite a
bit different from

00:29:33.576 --> 00:29:34.990
what it would be like
if you just trained and

00:29:34.991 --> 00:29:36.975
tested on clean text.

00:29:36.976 --> 00:29:40.370
So, excuse me, now let's
try to see if we can do,

00:29:40.371 --> 00:29:42.080
maybe, something
about robustness.

00:29:42.081 --> 00:29:43.945
So, what about
training with noise.

00:29:43.946 --> 00:29:46.430
So, there's way too many numbers,

00:29:46.431 --> 00:29:47.900
and so if you're
really bored we've

00:29:47.901 --> 00:29:49.570
just put charts and
charts on the paper.

00:29:49.571 --> 00:29:51.050
So, I'm going to
try to synthesize

00:29:51.051 --> 00:29:53.000
a couple of
quick lessons from it.

00:29:53.001 --> 00:29:55.625
So one is, if I
train on noisy text,

00:29:55.626 --> 00:30:00.710
but then I test on clean
unadulterated text, how do I do?

00:30:00.711 --> 00:30:02.665
So, here what we've done is,

00:30:02.666 --> 00:30:04.150
these are the different types

00:30:04.151 --> 00:30:05.540
of vanilla is completely clean,

00:30:05.541 --> 00:30:06.990
here are the different types
of noise that I

00:30:06.991 --> 00:30:08.140
mentioned earlier and here's

00:30:08.141 --> 00:30:08.815
what happens when I, sort of,

00:30:08.816 --> 00:30:12.720
ensemble some of the noise
and for French and Czech,

00:30:12.721 --> 00:30:14.340
so that this doesn't
get too cluttered.

00:30:14.341 --> 00:30:16.480
Here is sort of ideally
where we would like to

00:30:16.481 --> 00:30:20.445
be these lines and how well
the model is actually doing.

00:30:20.446 --> 00:30:23.420
The thing that I want
to point out is that

00:30:23.421 --> 00:30:24.820
the natural errors
are actually quite a

00:30:24.821 --> 00:30:26.610
bit harder for some reason.

00:30:26.611 --> 00:30:29.290
So, despite the fact that we,

00:30:29.291 --> 00:30:31.880
you know, this is insane, right?

00:30:31.881 --> 00:30:34.865
Here I've literally
scrambled the entire word,

00:30:34.866 --> 00:30:39.190
and that doesn't affect
the model as much when

00:30:39.191 --> 00:30:41.100
testing on the original data

00:30:41.101 --> 00:30:43.320
as when I introduce
natural errors.

00:30:43.321 --> 00:30:44.370
So, there's something that we

00:30:44.371 --> 00:30:45.850
still need to investigate there.

00:30:45.851 --> 00:30:47.200
Another thing though
that is somewhat

00:30:47.201 --> 00:30:48.860
heartening is that at

00:30:48.861 --> 00:30:50.100
least once you take the

00:30:50.101 --> 00:30:52.560
natural and you add
other noise to it,

00:30:52.561 --> 00:30:56.035
then the model becomes sort
of more generally robust.

00:30:56.036 --> 00:31:00.210
Another thing that I'll point
out very quickly is what

00:31:00.211 --> 00:31:03.800
happens if you test on
all different types of noise.

00:31:03.801 --> 00:31:06.640
So, here what I've done
is we've trained on

00:31:06.641 --> 00:31:08.030
a specific type of noise and then

00:31:08.031 --> 00:31:09.605
I'm presenting The Average Bleu,

00:31:09.606 --> 00:31:12.060
across five different types
of noise for testing.

00:31:12.061 --> 00:31:15.330
So, for instance, just
to make this concrete,

00:31:15.331 --> 00:31:18.710
here we just do the letter slop
inside of those words,

00:31:18.711 --> 00:31:22.055
but the bleu score here
is when you test on swap,

00:31:22.056 --> 00:31:23.660
middle, random keyboard
and natural.

00:31:23.661 --> 00:31:25.750
So, all of those
and then average.

00:31:25.751 --> 00:31:28.795
The first thing that I'd
like to point out is that

00:31:28.796 --> 00:31:32.470
the model does seem in
some sense to have a robustness.

00:31:32.471 --> 00:31:34.790
There's sort of the direction
of robustness, right?

00:31:34.791 --> 00:31:37.100
So, if a model is trained
on words that are

00:31:37.101 --> 00:31:39.460
totally randomized then it

00:31:39.461 --> 00:31:42.370
does better on average
including on things that

00:31:42.371 --> 00:31:46.790
were just these swaps than
if it sees less noise.

00:31:46.791 --> 00:31:48.020
This seems to sort

00:31:48.021 --> 00:31:51.135
of hold over to
the ensemble case, right?

00:31:51.136 --> 00:31:52.320
So, once I throw

00:31:52.321 --> 00:31:54.380
just tons and tons of
different noise at it,

00:31:54.381 --> 00:31:57.620
the model seems to do
the best sort of overall.

00:31:57.621 --> 00:32:00.295
All right. So, what
you would like to do.

00:32:00.296 --> 00:32:04.325
So, if I was amazing at
cognitive modeling somehow,

00:32:04.326 --> 00:32:05.950
I would build a model which not

00:32:05.951 --> 00:32:07.520
only took into account noise but

00:32:07.521 --> 00:32:11.140
also just like was impervious
to just didn't care.

00:32:11.141 --> 00:32:12.390
We tried a bunch of fancy things

00:32:12.391 --> 00:32:13.540
and they didn't
work and I can talk

00:32:13.541 --> 00:32:16.430
about self attention
E-type things later.

00:32:16.431 --> 00:32:19.460
So, what ended up
seeming to be fine was,

00:32:19.461 --> 00:32:21.690
let's just try the main
character representation.

00:32:21.691 --> 00:32:23.935
So, if I have a model

00:32:23.936 --> 00:32:25.530
whose word representation
is just literally

00:32:25.531 --> 00:32:27.540
the average of the vectors
for all the characters,

00:32:27.541 --> 00:32:29.720
then it doesn't matter
what order they're in.

00:32:29.721 --> 00:32:33.435
So, it's by definition
resilient to this.

00:32:33.436 --> 00:32:35.170
So, there is the good news.

00:32:35.171 --> 00:32:36.480
It means that I can train on

00:32:36.481 --> 00:32:38.855
completely clean
text with no errors.

00:32:38.856 --> 00:32:39.955
I can present it,

00:32:39.956 --> 00:32:44.120
completely scrambled text,
and it still does okay.

00:32:44.121 --> 00:32:46.835
So, I get 20 percent hit,
not the end of the world.

00:32:46.836 --> 00:32:48.330
Then the thing that's not

00:32:48.331 --> 00:32:50.090
surprising to anybody
in this room who's

00:32:50.091 --> 00:32:51.590
ever thought about
language is that there

00:32:51.591 --> 00:32:53.675
are morphologically
rich languages.

00:32:53.676 --> 00:32:56.240
For which turns out you

00:32:56.241 --> 00:32:58.715
really don't want to just
average all the characters,

00:32:58.716 --> 00:33:01.450
there is something in the
structure of the word.

00:33:01.451 --> 00:33:05.030
So, that's clearly
not the solution.

00:33:05.031 --> 00:33:06.860
As one final note though.

00:33:06.861 --> 00:33:07.935
So, I do want

00:33:07.936 --> 00:33:10.010
ideally to be able to
move in this direction.

00:33:10.011 --> 00:33:11.760
I'd like to be in
a situation where

00:33:11.761 --> 00:33:14.005
models are more
inherently robust.

00:33:14.006 --> 00:33:16.500
So, one thing we wanted
to do is at least analyze

00:33:16.501 --> 00:33:19.550
what the filters of
the model we're learning,

00:33:19.551 --> 00:33:22.225
when we're showing
various types of noise.

00:33:22.226 --> 00:33:24.150
So, it's a little bit hard
to interpret all of this,

00:33:24.151 --> 00:33:28.225
but let me just
focus on one point.

00:33:28.226 --> 00:33:31.610
This is what happens if you
look at the weights in all

00:33:31.611 --> 00:33:32.870
of the filters that are learned

00:33:32.871 --> 00:33:34.659
for the character embeddings,

00:33:34.660 --> 00:33:36.120
and our models when
they're trained in

00:33:36.121 --> 00:33:38.090
these four different settings,
with keyboard errors,

00:33:38.091 --> 00:33:40.670
natural errors,
completely randomized

00:33:40.671 --> 00:33:43.679
ordering of the letters in
the word and the ensemble.

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:45.740
Note that these have
a pretty high variance.

00:33:45.741 --> 00:33:46.720
So, you're learning different.

00:33:46.721 --> 00:33:48.290
So, different filters are
going to be firing for

00:33:48.291 --> 00:33:50.145
different settings, for
different environments.

00:33:50.146 --> 00:33:52.180
Then note that when you train on

00:33:52.181 --> 00:33:55.130
random, they've
completely collapsed.

00:33:55.131 --> 00:33:57.470
So, what we think
is happening here,

00:33:57.471 --> 00:33:58.580
is that basically when you

00:33:58.581 --> 00:34:00.380
completely randomize
the input like that,

00:34:00.381 --> 00:34:01.650
the models basically
rediscovering

00:34:01.651 --> 00:34:03.400
that mean character
model from before.

00:34:03.401 --> 00:34:05.755
It's basically giving up on
trying to learn structure.

00:34:05.756 --> 00:34:07.580
So, it just takes
some an average of

00:34:07.581 --> 00:34:09.750
everything that's in it
and then moves forward.

00:34:09.751 --> 00:34:12.840
So, that seems to be
a baseline default.

00:34:12.841 --> 00:34:14.620
Maybe we're even doing
something like that,

00:34:14.621 --> 00:34:17.595
but we have additional systems
which can trigger,

00:34:17.596 --> 00:34:18.725
if we're not understanding.

00:34:18.726 --> 00:34:19.960
So, we can reason about things,

00:34:19.961 --> 00:34:21.395
we can piece together

00:34:21.396 --> 00:34:23.345
how we might unscramble
award and so forth.

00:34:23.346 --> 00:34:25.190
So that's where we'd
like to move in

00:34:25.191 --> 00:34:28.190
terms of future research. Thanks.

00:34:36.470 --> 00:34:39.610
&gt;&gt; We have time for questions.

00:34:42.330 --> 00:34:46.360
&gt;&gt; Thanks for the great talk.
So, have you thought about

00:34:46.361 --> 00:34:49.830
what would be in the scenario
and adversarial noise?

00:34:49.831 --> 00:34:52.385
What would really
screw up the system?

00:34:52.386 --> 00:34:54.780
For the image
processing they will

00:34:54.781 --> 00:34:56.835
like random would
sometimes screw up.

00:34:56.836 --> 00:34:58.990
Is there like an adversarial
thing which will really.

00:34:58.991 --> 00:35:00.180
&gt;&gt; So, it depends
on what you mean.

00:35:00.181 --> 00:35:01.715
So It's two versions
of adversarial.

00:35:01.716 --> 00:35:03.820
So, one is can you break
all these systems?

00:35:03.821 --> 00:35:05.890
Obviously you can break
all these systems.

00:35:05.891 --> 00:35:08.030
What we didn't do that
we played around a

00:35:08.031 --> 00:35:10.230
little bit it's just
very time-consuming,

00:35:10.231 --> 00:35:13.520
there are cases, where if I
change one of the letters,

00:35:13.521 --> 00:35:15.130
I will change the translation.

00:35:15.131 --> 00:35:17.460
So, it's quite possible that,

00:35:17.461 --> 00:35:19.600
if I wanted to trick

00:35:19.601 --> 00:35:20.880
a translation system
for example into

00:35:20.881 --> 00:35:22.459
saying something in particular,

00:35:22.460 --> 00:35:24.860
I might be able to make
some changes on the input,

00:35:24.861 --> 00:35:26.820
which would cause that
but to which the human

00:35:26.821 --> 00:35:28.895
wouldn't think of
as affecting it.

00:35:28.896 --> 00:35:30.640
That's I think
the ideal adversarial case.

00:35:30.641 --> 00:35:32.880
I don't have
a great notion for it.

00:35:32.881 --> 00:35:34.010
We tried to do that manually,

00:35:34.011 --> 00:35:35.240
and we were able to
find some examples.

00:35:35.241 --> 00:35:37.210
I don't have
a pipeline as of yet,

00:35:37.211 --> 00:35:39.120
but I think that would be
awesome if you could come up

00:35:39.121 --> 00:35:42.540
with these examples. Yeah.

00:35:46.590 --> 00:35:48.930
&gt;&gt; Hi, great talk.

00:35:48.931 --> 00:35:50.400
I'm Engal from Boeing.

00:35:50.401 --> 00:35:53.870
We deal with a lot of
very noisy text data.

00:35:53.871 --> 00:35:56.745
For example, machine
is over the world,

00:35:56.746 --> 00:35:59.890
maintain the airplane,
and they write poorly.

00:35:59.891 --> 00:36:01.780
So, really interesting your talk.

00:36:01.781 --> 00:36:06.875
So, did you try to deal
with abbreviations,

00:36:06.876 --> 00:36:07.970
that chop off some of

00:36:07.971 --> 00:36:11.775
the letters and sometimes
they insert letters too.

00:36:11.776 --> 00:36:13.360
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, we didn't

00:36:13.361 --> 00:36:16.160
explicitly deal- I think
I actually skipped it.

00:36:16.161 --> 00:36:18.980
They're not exactly
abbreviations but

00:36:18.981 --> 00:36:20.480
definitely omissions is pretty

00:36:20.481 --> 00:36:22.725
common in the edit corpora.

00:36:22.726 --> 00:36:24.550
So, it is pretty
common for people to

00:36:24.551 --> 00:36:26.865
just completely miss
a letter for example.

00:36:26.866 --> 00:36:31.140
We're just starts to get
at this but no pursuit.

00:36:31.141 --> 00:36:34.130
Particularly for instance
in an abbreviation,

00:36:34.131 --> 00:36:35.675
you have even less to go on.

00:36:35.676 --> 00:36:39.250
So, I suspect they'll just
make everything even harder,

00:36:39.251 --> 00:36:41.000
but I don't have an answer to

00:36:41.001 --> 00:36:43.550
what to do about any of this.

00:36:43.551 --> 00:36:45.470
But, I don't.

00:36:45.471 --> 00:36:48.800
Yeah. That's a great problem.

00:36:48.801 --> 00:36:52.195
&gt;&gt; Also do you have
a sense how much

00:36:52.196 --> 00:36:56.660
misspelled words
involved before you

00:36:56.661 --> 00:36:58.580
are really not able to do as

00:36:58.581 --> 00:37:01.405
well percentage
wise in a sentence?

00:37:01.406 --> 00:37:04.945
&gt;&gt; Yes. So, it
becomes very clear.

00:37:04.946 --> 00:37:08.015
So, we all have our experiences
with machine translation.

00:37:08.016 --> 00:37:09.670
Where we run something
through and then

00:37:09.671 --> 00:37:13.035
we even the best of cases,

00:37:13.036 --> 00:37:14.610
we still have to do
some detective work to

00:37:14.611 --> 00:37:16.560
figure out what was
actually intended.

00:37:16.561 --> 00:37:19.020
So, in that sense, yeah.

00:37:19.021 --> 00:37:20.585
Even that 10 percent mark,

00:37:20.586 --> 00:37:23.995
if I'm just basically imagining
of a sentence and now

00:37:23.996 --> 00:37:27.790
two of those words are
something randomly new,

00:37:27.791 --> 00:37:29.070
the detective work becomes very,

00:37:29.071 --> 00:37:33.160
very difficult to figure out
what was actually going on.

00:37:33.180 --> 00:37:35.740
There's an example for
instance in the paper where we

00:37:35.741 --> 00:37:38.880
run one of these fully
scrambled things through

00:37:38.881 --> 00:37:42.070
Google Translate and also

00:37:42.071 --> 00:37:43.390
the output from
these other systems,

00:37:43.391 --> 00:37:44.840
you'd get out these sentences.

00:37:44.841 --> 00:37:48.010
In some cases, the models
default to copying.

00:37:48.011 --> 00:37:49.440
They're like, ''Oh,
well, I'll just take

00:37:49.441 --> 00:37:50.870
the German straight
through and just hand

00:37:50.871 --> 00:37:52.560
it back to you.''

00:37:52.561 --> 00:37:53.950
In some cases, you get

00:37:53.951 --> 00:37:56.095
what its attempt is
added a sentence.

00:37:56.096 --> 00:37:57.905
It's just that it's
completely meaningless.

00:37:57.906 --> 00:38:00.305
It has no bearing to what
was originally there.

00:38:00.306 --> 00:38:02.630
So, I think the answer
is probably not

00:38:02.631 --> 00:38:05.760
a lot and maybe a spellchecker
will be able to help you,

00:38:05.761 --> 00:38:07.650
but I also want to
note that there's

00:38:07.651 --> 00:38:09.815
a huge difference
between spellcheckers.

00:38:09.816 --> 00:38:11.110
So, there's a difference between,

00:38:11.111 --> 00:38:12.890
for instance,
the Google Translate,

00:38:12.891 --> 00:38:14.980
spell checking that we
were doing here and if you

00:38:14.981 --> 00:38:17.465
run like a spell on
your Unix machine.

00:38:17.466 --> 00:38:20.530
So, having some
sentential context

00:38:20.531 --> 00:38:21.660
is really important to being

00:38:21.661 --> 00:38:23.245
able to address those errors.

00:38:23.246 --> 00:38:26.290
Yeah.

00:38:26.291 --> 00:38:28.460
&gt;&gt; Okay. Any more questions?

00:38:33.910 --> 00:38:37.590
&gt;&gt; For your corpus
of natural errors,

00:38:37.591 --> 00:38:39.460
I noticed you had some that were

00:38:39.461 --> 00:38:42.370
the Wikipedia typos that

00:38:42.371 --> 00:38:43.840
were probably from
native speakers,

00:38:43.841 --> 00:38:46.000
but then you also had
language learners.

00:38:46.001 --> 00:38:46.540
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:38:46.541 --> 00:38:48.190
&gt;&gt; From a linguistic standpoint,

00:38:48.191 --> 00:38:49.630
the errors made by
language learners

00:38:49.631 --> 00:38:51.210
tend to be rather
different from native.

00:38:51.211 --> 00:38:53.090
So, I was wondering if
you looked into how

00:38:53.091 --> 00:38:56.060
those differ and
anything about that?

00:38:56.061 --> 00:38:58.540
&gt;&gt; No. Unfortunately
we were mostly

00:38:58.541 --> 00:39:00.565
focused on just
finding these errors,

00:39:00.566 --> 00:39:01.975
but you're absolutely right.

00:39:01.976 --> 00:39:07.090
I have to double-check
the details.

00:39:07.091 --> 00:39:08.730
I think in the case of
the German and the Czech,

00:39:08.731 --> 00:39:11.285
those are both language learners

00:39:11.286 --> 00:39:13.220
and then in the case
of French and

00:39:13.221 --> 00:39:15.015
English those are
Wikipedia edits.

00:39:15.016 --> 00:39:19.110
I think that's partially
just an issue- its availability.

00:39:19.111 --> 00:39:21.690
There just isn't as
much of that stuff.

00:39:21.691 --> 00:39:24.150
But, I think that that's
another fascinating thing.

00:39:24.151 --> 00:39:27.000
If we were to build these models
ideally that are robust,

00:39:27.001 --> 00:39:28.440
we'd like to be able to introduce

00:39:28.441 --> 00:39:31.090
these errors that require
someone to have first

00:39:31.091 --> 00:39:32.840
collected or found
all this signs of things

00:39:32.841 --> 00:39:35.160
and as the language

00:39:35.161 --> 00:39:41.100
becomes not English resource.
Got it. So, yeah.

00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:54.910
&gt;&gt; So, the third talk in
this session by Swabha.

00:39:54.911 --> 00:39:57.700
She will talk about
Syntactic Scaffolds

00:39:57.701 --> 00:39:59.980
for Semantic Structures.

00:39:59.981 --> 00:40:03.430
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Swabha.
Can you hear me?

00:40:03.431 --> 00:40:06.430
Okay. Yeah. I'll be talking

00:40:06.431 --> 00:40:09.440
about Syntactic Scaffolds
for Semantic Structures,

00:40:09.441 --> 00:40:13.365
and this is a work done jointly
with my co-authors, Sam,

00:40:13.366 --> 00:40:15.340
who's right here, and Quentin,

00:40:15.341 --> 00:40:17.595
Luke, Chris, and Noah,
who's right there.

00:40:17.596 --> 00:40:21.975
Lots of affiliations,
literally, too many logos.

00:40:21.976 --> 00:40:24.670
All right. So, the task we're

00:40:24.671 --> 00:40:27.555
interested in is
Semantic Structure Prediction.

00:40:27.556 --> 00:40:29.585
Now, depending on the formalism,

00:40:29.586 --> 00:40:32.370
the semantic structures
may look very different.

00:40:32.371 --> 00:40:34.130
For example, we
have the sentence,

00:40:34.131 --> 00:40:37.245
Ivanka told Fei-Fei
that she is inspiring.

00:40:37.246 --> 00:40:40.420
The frame semantics structure

00:40:40.421 --> 00:40:42.670
for this sentence
looks like this.

00:40:42.671 --> 00:40:44.875
There are two frames
of meaning, one,

00:40:44.876 --> 00:40:47.545
about the event
telling and another

00:40:47.546 --> 00:40:51.495
about the event
subjective influence.

00:40:51.496 --> 00:40:52.870
For the telling frame,

00:40:52.871 --> 00:40:54.380
the speaker is Ivanka,

00:40:54.381 --> 00:40:55.945
the addressee is Fei-Fei,

00:40:55.946 --> 00:40:59.375
and the message is
that she's inspiring.

00:40:59.376 --> 00:41:03.280
For the subjective
influence frame,

00:41:03.281 --> 00:41:04.750
the entity is she,

00:41:04.751 --> 00:41:07.615
and she is providing
the influence.

00:41:07.616 --> 00:41:12.360
So, this is what the frame
semantic structures look like.

00:41:12.361 --> 00:41:16.795
A different kind of structure
is given by coreference,

00:41:16.796 --> 00:41:18.635
where you have in green,

00:41:18.636 --> 00:41:21.395
all the entities which are
present in the sentence,

00:41:21.396 --> 00:41:23.140
as well as relationships

00:41:23.141 --> 00:41:26.505
between the word she and Fei-Fei.

00:41:26.506 --> 00:41:29.960
Hopefully, she refers to
Fei-Fei and not Ivanka.

00:41:29.961 --> 00:41:33.230
Yeah. So, this is the kind of

00:41:33.231 --> 00:41:36.720
structure that you get
from Coreference chains.

00:41:36.721 --> 00:41:39.680
So, as you can see these
structures look very different.

00:41:39.681 --> 00:41:41.480
The frame semantics looks quite

00:41:41.481 --> 00:41:43.790
different from the
coref structure,

00:41:43.791 --> 00:41:48.430
but what they have in common
is the syntactic framework.

00:41:48.431 --> 00:41:50.730
So, for example, we

00:41:50.731 --> 00:41:56.385
have the constituency tree
for the same sentence,

00:41:56.386 --> 00:41:57.940
and we can see that

00:41:57.941 --> 00:42:01.105
the different arguments
speaker is a noun phrase,

00:42:01.106 --> 00:42:02.830
addressee is also a noun- phrase,

00:42:02.831 --> 00:42:05.535
and the message is in S bar.

00:42:05.536 --> 00:42:08.365
So, what you can see
from here is that,

00:42:08.366 --> 00:42:11.105
if you have the syntactic
information of a sentence,

00:42:11.106 --> 00:42:15.250
it might be easy to get to the
semantics of the sentence.

00:42:15.251 --> 00:42:20.805
Indeed, this has been tried
extensively in previous work.

00:42:20.806 --> 00:42:25.185
So, prior work involves
using a pipeline,

00:42:25.186 --> 00:42:26.440
where given a sentence,

00:42:26.441 --> 00:42:29.070
you first do
the syntactic processing

00:42:29.071 --> 00:42:31.800
of the sentence to get
the full syntactic tree.

00:42:31.801 --> 00:42:34.345
Use features from that tree to

00:42:34.346 --> 00:42:37.840
extract the semantic
graphs from it.

00:42:37.841 --> 00:42:40.565
Now, the problem
with this is that,

00:42:40.566 --> 00:42:43.495
it is both very expensive,

00:42:43.496 --> 00:42:46.220
and since you're doing
things in a pipeline,

00:42:46.221 --> 00:42:49.640
you're very likely
to cascade errors.

00:42:49.641 --> 00:42:50.940
If you get the syntax wrong,

00:42:50.941 --> 00:42:53.450
you're very likely to also
get the semantics wrong.

00:42:53.451 --> 00:42:55.110
Also, the tree that we saw in

00:42:55.111 --> 00:42:57.625
the previous example is
way more complicated.

00:42:57.626 --> 00:42:59.010
Whereas, the semantics was

00:42:59.011 --> 00:43:01.015
relatively simple
like Jonathan said,

00:43:01.016 --> 00:43:02.975
when people read sentences,

00:43:02.976 --> 00:43:04.760
if they are trying
to semantically

00:43:04.761 --> 00:43:05.800
understand the sentences

00:43:05.801 --> 00:43:08.370
they did not read through
the entire sentence.

00:43:08.371 --> 00:43:09.595
Some words can be skipped.

00:43:09.596 --> 00:43:12.275
Some words are semantically
vacuous, and so on.

00:43:12.276 --> 00:43:15.820
So, using syntax in
a pipeline works,

00:43:15.821 --> 00:43:19.160
but it is probably not
the best solution.

00:43:19.161 --> 00:43:22.370
Now, in recent times,

00:43:22.371 --> 00:43:25.830
people have come up with
neural end-to-end models,

00:43:25.831 --> 00:43:28.804
which have completely
done away with syntax.

00:43:28.805 --> 00:43:32.335
So, these end-to-end models
just start with the sentence

00:43:32.336 --> 00:43:33.540
and try to predict

00:43:33.541 --> 00:43:36.565
the entire semantic graph
from the sentence.

00:43:36.566 --> 00:43:39.400
Now, even though these models

00:43:39.401 --> 00:43:43.795
work regardless of
not using syntax,

00:43:43.796 --> 00:43:45.820
it has not been conclusively

00:43:45.821 --> 00:43:48.710
shown that syntax does
not help further.

00:43:48.711 --> 00:43:53.185
In fact, people from UW
[inaudible] , somewhere over here,

00:43:53.186 --> 00:43:55.710
showed in her paper that using

00:43:55.711 --> 00:44:00.035
constraints from
gold syntax actually helps.

00:44:00.036 --> 00:44:03.480
So, there is still
hope for syntax.

00:44:03.481 --> 00:44:04.735
There is actually a lot of hope.

00:44:04.736 --> 00:44:06.510
So, what we are suggesting in

00:44:06.511 --> 00:44:09.940
this work is somewhere in
between these two worlds.

00:44:09.941 --> 00:44:11.420
We wanted to use syntax,

00:44:11.421 --> 00:44:15.015
but we don't want to go
through this whole process of

00:44:15.016 --> 00:44:19.280
pipelining and errors
cascading and so on.

00:44:19.281 --> 00:44:22.065
So, what we are suggesting is

00:44:22.066 --> 00:44:26.455
this method called Syntactic
Scaffolds, which users,

00:44:26.456 --> 00:44:29.850
as the name suggest
syntax but does

00:44:29.851 --> 00:44:34.260
not go through
the whole pipelining shrine.

00:44:34.300 --> 00:44:37.525
Hopefully, I introduce
the problem to you.

00:44:37.526 --> 00:44:40.880
Let's look in detail what
these Syntactic Scaffolds are.

00:44:40.881 --> 00:44:44.770
So, this is essentially
a multi-task learning setup,

00:44:44.771 --> 00:44:46.690
where one of the tasks is

00:44:46.691 --> 00:44:48.720
the primary task we
are interested in,

00:44:48.721 --> 00:44:51.005
which is the semantic
structure prediction does,

00:44:51.006 --> 00:44:53.600
frame semantics or
coref in our case,

00:44:53.601 --> 00:44:56.485
and the second task is
syntactic prediction.

00:44:56.486 --> 00:44:58.875
However, in contrast to

00:44:58.876 --> 00:45:01.385
your traditional
multi-task learning setup,

00:45:01.386 --> 00:45:04.565
where both the tasks
are equally important.

00:45:04.566 --> 00:45:06.815
For us, the syntactic task

00:45:06.816 --> 00:45:08.930
is a secondary auxiliary tasks,

00:45:08.931 --> 00:45:10.430
and the primary task is

00:45:10.431 --> 00:45:12.750
the semantic tasks that
we're interested in.

00:45:12.751 --> 00:45:16.680
So, multitask learning
gives us this advantage,

00:45:16.681 --> 00:45:20.340
that we can use
shared parameters to learn

00:45:20.341 --> 00:45:23.640
better contextualized
representations for the spans and

00:45:23.641 --> 00:45:29.040
whatever substructures that
are semantically non-raculous.

00:45:29.041 --> 00:45:31.830
Also, in this setup,

00:45:31.831 --> 00:45:33.840
as an channeled
multitask learning,

00:45:33.841 --> 00:45:37.045
we do not need the supervision
for the semantics

00:45:37.046 --> 00:45:40.575
and syntax to come on
from the same data set.

00:45:40.576 --> 00:45:42.630
So, we can use
two different data sets,

00:45:42.631 --> 00:45:45.495
one with only semantic
annotations and other with

00:45:45.496 --> 00:45:49.535
only syntactic annotations
and still be able to learn,

00:45:49.536 --> 00:45:53.800
which is difficult in
your regular pipeline setup,

00:45:53.801 --> 00:45:55.710
because you need the syntax on

00:45:55.711 --> 00:45:59.785
the same data before you can
predict semantics on it.

00:45:59.786 --> 00:46:02.490
Another thing that the

00:46:02.491 --> 00:46:05.070
syntactic scaffolds
approach offers us,

00:46:05.071 --> 00:46:07.070
is that we do not have to predict

00:46:07.071 --> 00:46:09.750
this entire complicated
syntactic tree

00:46:09.751 --> 00:46:11.800
with all its bells and whistles.

00:46:11.801 --> 00:46:13.730
We just have to focus on

00:46:13.731 --> 00:46:16.660
the substructures in
that syntactic tree,

00:46:16.661 --> 00:46:20.345
which are meaningful for
the semantic prediction task.

00:46:20.346 --> 00:46:24.485
We'll look at some of
the substructures later on.

00:46:24.486 --> 00:46:29.130
Finally, as the name
suggests, it's a scaffold,

00:46:29.131 --> 00:46:32.050
so it is only used at
learning time to learn

00:46:32.051 --> 00:46:36.430
the model with all the
syntactic information in it,

00:46:36.431 --> 00:46:37.750
and at test time,

00:46:37.751 --> 00:46:42.190
we can totally discard
this and move on.

00:46:42.191 --> 00:46:45.390
All right. So,
the learning problem

00:46:45.391 --> 00:46:47.045
looks something like this.

00:46:47.046 --> 00:46:49.180
So, this is the
objective equation,

00:46:49.181 --> 00:46:50.940
one shows us the objective.

00:46:50.941 --> 00:46:54.450
The first term in the objective
is the primary objective,

00:46:54.451 --> 00:46:57.055
the syntactic structure
prediction objective,

00:46:57.056 --> 00:46:59.425
which we will look into later.

00:46:59.426 --> 00:47:02.525
The secondary objective is
the scaffold objective,

00:47:02.526 --> 00:47:04.800
both are interpolated
by this term,

00:47:04.801 --> 00:47:07.605
delta, which is
a mixing ratio that

00:47:07.606 --> 00:47:11.815
tells you how do we relate
these two objectives.

00:47:11.816 --> 00:47:14.770
So, all you have to do is plug-in

00:47:14.771 --> 00:47:20.370
the scaffolding objective
into your semantic objective,

00:47:20.371 --> 00:47:22.020
and you will be able to

00:47:22.021 --> 00:47:27.335
learn information from
the syntactic tree bank.

00:47:27.336 --> 00:47:30.570
If you look into
the objective, what it does,

00:47:30.571 --> 00:47:33.635
it's a simple logistic
regression classifier.

00:47:33.636 --> 00:47:36.630
It takes all possible spans in

00:47:36.631 --> 00:47:39.670
the sentence up to
a certain length and

00:47:39.671 --> 00:47:42.025
tries to predict
for each span what

00:47:42.026 --> 00:47:45.820
the syntactic substructure
of that span could be,

00:47:45.821 --> 00:47:47.900
whether or not it's
a noun phrase,

00:47:47.901 --> 00:47:49.490
what kind of noun phrase it is,

00:47:49.491 --> 00:47:53.115
what the parent of the noun
phrase could be, and so on.

00:47:53.116 --> 00:47:55.090
So to formalize it,

00:47:55.091 --> 00:48:00.470
the different substructures
we tried to predict,

00:48:00.471 --> 00:48:04.065
Phrase Identity, whether
or not a span is

00:48:04.066 --> 00:48:07.800
a constituent phrase
or a syntactic phrase,

00:48:07.801 --> 00:48:09.915
the phrase type of the span.

00:48:09.916 --> 00:48:13.440
If the span is not
a syntactic phrase,

00:48:13.441 --> 00:48:16.650
we predict null, the

00:48:16.651 --> 00:48:21.515
phrase type and the parent
phrase type of the span.

00:48:21.516 --> 00:48:25.235
This parent phrase type
is the constituent that

00:48:25.236 --> 00:48:28.400
immediately precedes it in

00:48:28.401 --> 00:48:32.060
the tree and
special phrase types,

00:48:32.061 --> 00:48:34.230
such as noun phrase and
prepositional phrase.

00:48:34.231 --> 00:48:35.505
That is given a span,

00:48:35.506 --> 00:48:36.660
whether or not, it is

00:48:36.661 --> 00:48:38.460
a noun phrase or
a prepositional phrase.

00:48:38.461 --> 00:48:41.470
We do this because a
lot of these arguments,

00:48:41.471 --> 00:48:43.670
as we saw the names,
Ivanka and Fei-Fei.

00:48:43.671 --> 00:48:44.915
They were noun phrases,

00:48:44.916 --> 00:48:46.270
and a lot of and

00:48:46.271 --> 00:48:49.365
semantic arguments tend
to be noun phrases.

00:48:49.366 --> 00:48:51.220
A lot of semantic
arguments tend to

00:48:51.221 --> 00:48:53.870
be prepositional
phrases and so on.

00:48:54.560 --> 00:48:58.335
Okay. So, let's look at some of

00:48:58.336 --> 00:49:00.910
the the two downstream tasks that

00:49:00.911 --> 00:49:04.020
we incorporated
this framework in.

00:49:04.021 --> 00:49:06.180
The first one is Frame-Semantics.

00:49:06.181 --> 00:49:07.960
So, again, this is what

00:49:07.961 --> 00:49:09.775
a Frame-Semantic graph
looks like.

00:49:09.776 --> 00:49:12.814
We are using the FrameNet corpus,

00:49:12.815 --> 00:49:17.455
which was created at Berkeley.

00:49:17.456 --> 00:49:20.040
In this particular work,

00:49:20.041 --> 00:49:22.495
we only focus on the task

00:49:22.496 --> 00:49:25.500
of identifying and
labeling these spans.

00:49:25.501 --> 00:49:27.085
So, given a sentence,

00:49:27.086 --> 00:49:30.100
as well as the frames
in the sentence,

00:49:30.101 --> 00:49:32.285
that is if we know that there is

00:49:32.286 --> 00:49:34.660
a frame telling in the sentence,

00:49:34.661 --> 00:49:36.310
and if you know there is a frame

00:49:36.311 --> 00:49:38.600
subjective influence
in the sentence,

00:49:38.601 --> 00:49:40.745
are we able to predict what

00:49:40.746 --> 00:49:43.780
the arguments for
those frames are?

00:49:43.781 --> 00:49:49.660
This frame identification and
also, target identification

00:49:49.661 --> 00:49:53.056
tasks are relatively
straightforward,

00:49:53.057 --> 00:49:56.450
and have been solved previously
with high accuracy but

00:49:56.451 --> 00:49:58.560
argument labeling and argument

00:49:58.561 --> 00:50:00.470
identification on
difficult tasks,

00:50:00.471 --> 00:50:02.890
so we focus only on that.

00:50:03.930 --> 00:50:09.640
As a baseline, we use this model
called Semi-Markov CRFs,

00:50:09.641 --> 00:50:12.830
which is a
generalization of a CRF.

00:50:12.831 --> 00:50:15.920
So, the generalization only
comes from the simple fact,

00:50:15.921 --> 00:50:19.190
that you have to predict
for a given sentence.

00:50:19.191 --> 00:50:21.660
You predict a segmentation
of the sentence.

00:50:21.661 --> 00:50:23.295
So, in a general CRF,

00:50:23.296 --> 00:50:26.010
each segment is of
the same fixed length,

00:50:26.011 --> 00:50:27.515
which is typically one.

00:50:27.516 --> 00:50:31.035
A Semi-Markov CRF
offers you the freedom,

00:50:31.036 --> 00:50:33.330
wherein the length modelling,

00:50:33.331 --> 00:50:35.870
also the length of the segment
that you're predicting.

00:50:35.871 --> 00:50:38.370
So, this works very well
for our case because

00:50:38.371 --> 00:50:41.345
arguments can have
arbitrary lengths.

00:50:41.346 --> 00:50:43.100
So, this is a natural fit

00:50:43.101 --> 00:50:47.150
for the task of frames
semantic parsing.

00:50:47.151 --> 00:50:50.380
Without going into
too many details,

00:50:50.381 --> 00:50:56.425
I'll just say that we focus
on spans up to length D. So,

00:50:56.426 --> 00:50:57.765
if you had to model

00:50:57.766 --> 00:51:01.240
every possible span in
the sentence of length N,

00:51:01.241 --> 00:51:04.510
you would run into O of
N square complexity,

00:51:04.511 --> 00:51:07.090
and we pare it down to O of ND

00:51:07.091 --> 00:51:09.980
because we only limit
ourselves to spans of

00:51:09.981 --> 00:51:13.740
length D. The factor L comes from

00:51:13.741 --> 00:51:16.520
the different possible labels

00:51:16.521 --> 00:51:20.055
that you could label a span with.

00:51:20.056 --> 00:51:24.990
All right and to model
the spans themselves,

00:51:24.991 --> 00:51:27.170
we use an architecture like this.

00:51:27.171 --> 00:51:31.740
This is from coauthors,
Canton and Luke.

00:51:31.741 --> 00:51:35.970
So, how this model works
is given a sentence,

00:51:35.971 --> 00:51:38.020
we first get the
pretrained embeddings

00:51:38.021 --> 00:51:40.295
for that sentence
we use clav here,

00:51:40.296 --> 00:51:44.945
we pass it through
two bi-directional LSTMs to get

00:51:44.946 --> 00:51:48.515
the contextualized
representations of

00:51:48.516 --> 00:51:50.205
each token in the sentence.

00:51:50.206 --> 00:51:53.075
These are given by
the yellow embeddings

00:51:53.076 --> 00:51:56.440
and to represent a span
what we do is we take

00:51:56.441 --> 00:51:58.670
the first word in
the span and the last

00:51:58.671 --> 00:52:01.635
word and concatenate
the hidden representation of

00:52:01.636 --> 00:52:05.515
both words and we also use

00:52:05.516 --> 00:52:10.260
this head finding mechanism
that finds the head or it

00:52:10.261 --> 00:52:13.090
uses a simple attention
mechanism to figure

00:52:13.091 --> 00:52:16.300
out what word in the span carries

00:52:16.301 --> 00:52:21.060
the most syntactic information
and that is given by

00:52:21.061 --> 00:52:22.910
the red nodes over there and

00:52:22.911 --> 00:52:24.630
the final span representation

00:52:24.631 --> 00:52:27.835
is given as the green vectors.

00:52:27.836 --> 00:52:30.730
Okay, so all we do in

00:52:30.731 --> 00:52:35.460
the scaffolded Frame SRL
is plug-in this objective.

00:52:35.461 --> 00:52:38.170
So, the primary objective
as shown on top,

00:52:38.171 --> 00:52:42.600
it is a modified
log-likelihood objective

00:52:42.601 --> 00:52:45.000
and all we do now is use

00:52:45.001 --> 00:52:46.770
the same span representations or

00:52:46.771 --> 00:52:48.680
use the same parameters to learn

00:52:48.681 --> 00:52:50.530
these span representations and

00:52:50.531 --> 00:52:53.190
plug-in the
scaffolding objective.

00:52:53.191 --> 00:52:58.905
A scaffolding objective is
trained on two nodes data,

00:52:58.906 --> 00:53:01.445
which is built on
top of Penn Treebank

00:53:01.446 --> 00:53:05.495
and the primary objective
is trained on freeman.

00:53:05.496 --> 00:53:09.160
So, here since we have
two different corpora

00:53:09.161 --> 00:53:14.580
we can get by even though
there is no data overlap.

00:53:14.581 --> 00:53:17.605
All right, so, looking
at some results

00:53:17.606 --> 00:53:21.445
our baseline semi-CRF model gets

00:53:21.446 --> 00:53:24.780
a significant improvement
over previous state of

00:53:24.781 --> 00:53:27.065
the art models and

00:53:27.066 --> 00:53:30.005
as we add these different
scaffolding models,

00:53:30.006 --> 00:53:32.180
we keep on seeing improvements.

00:53:32.181 --> 00:53:34.980
So, this shows that adding
syntactic information is

00:53:34.981 --> 00:53:38.050
still useful and
the best model that we have,

00:53:38.051 --> 00:53:39.745
which is shown in gray

00:53:39.746 --> 00:53:43.455
is this particular
scaffolding model,

00:53:43.456 --> 00:53:46.260
which takes a span and
predicts whether or

00:53:46.261 --> 00:53:49.535
not it should be a noun phrase
or a prepositional phrase.

00:53:49.536 --> 00:53:53.680
So, getting very
specific seem to help.

00:53:53.681 --> 00:53:57.730
All right, I'll quickly
go over the next task,

00:53:57.731 --> 00:53:59.290
which is coreference resolution,

00:53:59.291 --> 00:54:02.280
this is also a span-based task.

00:54:02.281 --> 00:54:06.870
So, we use this model
from Canton and others

00:54:06.871 --> 00:54:09.375
where this task is treated as

00:54:09.376 --> 00:54:12.060
a series of sequence
classification tasks.

00:54:12.061 --> 00:54:14.615
So, spans in
the sentence are labeled

00:54:14.616 --> 00:54:18.390
as entities and also you

00:54:18.391 --> 00:54:20.760
label for a given entity what is

00:54:20.761 --> 00:54:24.890
the antecedent entity to
that particular entity.

00:54:24.891 --> 00:54:27.550
So, this way, you can build

00:54:27.551 --> 00:54:29.580
these simple
classification decisions

00:54:29.581 --> 00:54:32.400
to get entity clusters.

00:54:32.780 --> 00:54:36.310
So, again, all we do is since

00:54:36.311 --> 00:54:38.400
we are doing
a span-based modelling

00:54:38.401 --> 00:54:39.695
for the coreference model,

00:54:39.696 --> 00:54:42.790
we can use the same
span representation

00:54:42.791 --> 00:54:45.685
to make the classification
of what kind

00:54:45.686 --> 00:54:52.045
of syntactic span it is and
so it's again very simple,

00:54:52.046 --> 00:54:55.995
just plug in and interpolate
with this delta.

00:54:55.996 --> 00:54:59.420
Again, we see an improvement
when we plug in

00:54:59.421 --> 00:55:03.070
the scaffolding objective
and the improvement is

00:55:03.071 --> 00:55:05.010
not as much as we

00:55:05.011 --> 00:55:07.300
saw in the frame net task
and this could be

00:55:07.301 --> 00:55:09.400
attributed to the fact that we do

00:55:09.401 --> 00:55:11.640
this again on the
onto notes task,

00:55:11.641 --> 00:55:17.010
where the way
the coreference mentions

00:55:17.011 --> 00:55:19.165
were annotated were on top

00:55:19.166 --> 00:55:21.380
of these syntactic constituents.

00:55:21.381 --> 00:55:22.750
So, you already have

00:55:22.751 --> 00:55:25.220
that information in
the data and you're

00:55:25.221 --> 00:55:30.365
reusing the data to learn
these syntactic constituents.

00:55:30.366 --> 00:55:32.370
So, it is possible that,

00:55:32.371 --> 00:55:35.630
that is why the improvement
is not as much.

00:55:35.631 --> 00:55:37.650
So, in summary, we saw that

00:55:37.651 --> 00:55:39.790
syntax is very useful
for semantics,

00:55:39.791 --> 00:55:41.520
but syntax is expensive,

00:55:41.521 --> 00:55:47.150
so scaffolds are
an inexpensive alternative

00:55:47.151 --> 00:55:50.715
that can get the
best of both worlds.

00:55:50.716 --> 00:55:53.980
Okay, so what's next so
this is not something

00:55:53.981 --> 00:55:56.900
that is confined to

00:55:56.901 --> 00:56:00.480
only semantic tasks
basically any task that uses

00:56:00.481 --> 00:56:02.990
syntactic span information could

00:56:02.991 --> 00:56:05.630
benefit from such scaffolding.

00:56:05.631 --> 00:56:08.710
Syntactic dependencies
could be used as

00:56:08.711 --> 00:56:11.050
scaffolds too and finally,

00:56:11.051 --> 00:56:13.260
you can use semantics
as scaffolds

00:56:13.261 --> 00:56:15.760
for various downstream tasks.

00:56:15.761 --> 00:56:18.020
So, that's all.

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:26.480
&gt;&gt; Question.

00:56:31.140 --> 00:56:33.680
&gt;&gt; There is one there.

00:56:37.350 --> 00:56:40.390
&gt;&gt; Can I ask
the differences between

00:56:40.391 --> 00:56:44.400
the recurrent neural
network together with

00:56:44.401 --> 00:56:47.470
the classification on top of it

00:56:47.471 --> 00:56:51.105
and the recursive neural
network, standard recursive.

00:56:51.106 --> 00:56:56.480
&gt;&gt; Right. So, you can think
of recursive neural nets

00:56:56.481 --> 00:57:02.460
as like a special case of
recurrent neural networks.

00:57:02.461 --> 00:57:06.910
So, basically to build
a recursive neural net,

00:57:06.911 --> 00:57:09.010
you need to know
what structure it is

00:57:09.011 --> 00:57:11.370
modelling for which
you need to know

00:57:11.371 --> 00:57:14.930
that there is some kind of
a syntactic tree and you will

00:57:14.931 --> 00:57:19.390
use that syntactic tree to
compose together the nodes,

00:57:19.391 --> 00:57:22.175
but here we are just
modeling the sentences,

00:57:22.176 --> 00:57:24.815
we don't have any information
about structure.

00:57:24.816 --> 00:57:26.550
So, we cannot really use

00:57:26.551 --> 00:57:29.255
recursive neural nets
in this case,

00:57:29.256 --> 00:57:33.400
but it is another way to
represent information

00:57:33.401 --> 00:57:35.930
like these graphical information

00:57:35.931 --> 00:57:38.780
or tree-specific information.

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:45.495
&gt;&gt; So, great presentation.

00:57:45.496 --> 00:57:50.620
If you wanted to enhance
where you are going beyond

00:57:50.621 --> 00:57:53.920
a frame induction and capture

00:57:53.921 --> 00:57:56.090
more semantics like
possession and

00:57:56.091 --> 00:57:59.285
negation and some of
these other types of things,

00:57:59.286 --> 00:58:03.405
would you need to
go more into doing

00:58:03.406 --> 00:58:05.510
grammatical dependency types of

00:58:05.511 --> 00:58:06.860
relationships rather than

00:58:06.861 --> 00:58:08.450
these span-based representations.

00:58:08.451 --> 00:58:11.070
&gt;&gt; Yeah, of course. So, we've

00:58:11.071 --> 00:58:12.940
actually been thinking about

00:58:12.941 --> 00:58:15.145
doing similar things for

00:58:15.146 --> 00:58:18.200
these national language
inference-based tasks,

00:58:18.201 --> 00:58:22.100
where relationships
between words is

00:58:22.101 --> 00:58:26.640
shown to be enough to predict
inference relationships

00:58:26.641 --> 00:58:32.130
between these hypotheses and
premises and that is where

00:58:32.131 --> 00:58:36.900
the syntactic dependency
scaffolds is hoped to help

00:58:36.901 --> 00:58:42.370
the most because yeah,

00:58:42.371 --> 00:58:43.850
it really depends on the task.

00:58:43.851 --> 00:58:46.740
So, here we had
two span-based tasks and

00:58:46.741 --> 00:58:50.365
so we saw that these span-based
scaffolding things help,

00:58:50.366 --> 00:58:52.880
but it really depends
on the end task,

00:58:52.881 --> 00:58:55.260
so you typically if

00:58:55.261 --> 00:58:57.540
you wanted to do
very good multitask learning,

00:58:57.541 --> 00:58:59.260
you typically want
to learn structures,

00:58:59.261 --> 00:59:00.810
which are kind of similar,

00:59:00.811 --> 00:59:02.430
but there is nothing
to say that if

00:59:02.431 --> 00:59:04.760
you learn from
different structures,

00:59:04.761 --> 00:59:06.505
you can't get anything.

00:59:06.506 --> 00:59:09.565
I don't have experimental
results to show that,

00:59:09.566 --> 00:59:11.890
but it's also possible.

00:59:11.891 --> 00:59:13.580
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:59:18.660 --> 00:59:22.700
&gt;&gt; I was just curious
if you've done only POS

00:59:22.701 --> 00:59:27.335
tagging as the other objective
instead of constituency.

00:59:27.336 --> 00:59:29.890
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so that's
a good question.

00:59:29.891 --> 00:59:34.275
So, there has been related
work that does POS

00:59:34.276 --> 00:59:36.675
tagging as one of

00:59:36.676 --> 00:59:39.665
the tasks in this multitask
learning scenario.

00:59:39.666 --> 00:59:43.480
We did not do that
because we were

00:59:43.481 --> 00:59:45.940
more interested in these

00:59:45.941 --> 00:59:48.515
more complicated
structural tasks,

00:59:48.516 --> 00:59:51.180
but people have used POS as

00:59:51.181 --> 00:59:54.195
one of the maybe the
auxiliary part of

00:59:54.196 --> 00:59:57.045
the scaffolding tasks that
does work from you have

00:59:57.046 --> 00:59:58.640
Goldberg and others who have

00:59:58.641 --> 01:00:01.450
done similar things
and saw improvements,

01:00:01.451 --> 01:00:05.520
but yeah that's also
another alternative.

01:00:05.521 --> 01:00:07.420
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

